From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Crunch41 <70764418+Crunch41@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:31:37 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] Validate packets and enforce room admission

The room trusted every packet it received and every peer that connected,
and gave moderator rights to anyone who claimed the owner's nickname.

Packets:

- Empty packets are dropped before dispatch; the event loop read
  data[0] of every packet, also of empty ones.
- Chat, game info, join, kick, ban and unban packets that fail to parse
  are dropped with a warning instead of being used half-read.
- Proxy and LDN packets must hold their whole header (17 and 11 bytes),
  and are dropped above 1536 bytes. Nintendo's Pia sends at most about
  1472 bytes of UDP payload, plus up to 21 bytes of room header, so real
  game traffic passes; the cap bounds what one packet can make the room
  broadcast to everyone.
- EDEN_ROOM_RELAY_BUDGET_KBPS (default 0 = off) limits how many relay
  bytes per second one member may send. Each relayed packet fans out to
  every other member, so this bounds the egress one member can cause.
  Packets over the budget are dropped for the rest of that second, with
  one warning per second. Too low a value drops real game traffic, which
  is why it is off by default.
- An exception in a handler is logged and the loop continues, instead of
  ending the room thread and the room.

Admission (password, bans, capacity, nickname, network_version) happens
in HandleJoinRequest, but the ENet connection that carries the join
request exists before it:

- Proxy and LDN packets were relayed for any connected peer, so a client
  could skip the join request, and with it every admission check, and
  still send gameplay traffic to all members. They are now relayed only
  for peers in the member list.
- A rejected peer (wrong password, room full, name or IP collision,
  version mismatch, banned) kept its ENet slot until the client gave up,
  up to the peer timeout. Each rejection now ends with
  enet_peer_disconnect_later, which frees the slot once the rejection has
  been delivered. SendUserBanned is shared with the moderator ban, which
  already disconnects, so the two ban checks in HandleJoinRequest
  disconnect at the call site.
- Join requests are limited to one per IP address per second; faster
  attempts are disconnected without an answer. Addresses idle for 10
  minutes are forgotten. Players behind one NAT address who join within
  the same second have to retry.
- HandleClientDisconnection called enet_peer_disconnect on a peer that
  ENet had already reset before reporting the disconnect, which did
  nothing. The call is removed.

Version mismatches are still rejected exactly as upstream does.

Moderators: upstream gives moderator rights (kick, ban, unban, ban list)
to anyone whose nickname equals the room's lobby account name, without
any token, and to verified lobby moderators and the room owner, from
anywhere. For a self-hosted public room, moderation should stay with the
person running it:

- Every moderator check first requires the peer's address to be on the
  local network: 10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16 or 127/8. Remote peers are
  never moderators, whatever their nickname or token says.
- From a local address, the moderator is a verified lobby moderator, the
  host nickname, or the user named by EDEN_ROOM_MOD_USERNAME (matched
  against the verified username or the nickname), which defaults to the
  room's lobby account (--username).

Compatibility: lobby moderators lose their rights in this room when they
join from outside the local network. A room behind a reverse proxy or
port forward that rewrites source addresses to a local one would treat
everyone as local; publish the port directly.

Settings are read once, when the room is created.

Tests: tests/test_room_protocol.py sends empty, truncated, oversized and
malformed packets and checks they are not relayed and that relay keeps
working; checks that a peer that never joined cannot relay, that wrong
password and version are rejected and the peer disconnected, that a
second join within a second is refused, that a nickname and slot can be
reused after a disconnect, and that the player named by
EDEN_ROOM_MOD_USERNAME is a moderator (JoinSuccessAsMod) and another is
not (a kick request is denied).
